00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.310
Hello, and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.440
And today we're going to be looking
at lipids and specifically base

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:17.259
hydrolysis, which that's all
about saponification, meaning that

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:18.270
we're going to be making soap.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.190
So how does this work?

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:23.969
Well, if you have a lipid or a fat
that you have from, maybe like the

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.090
store or from different animals
or all, all kinds of different

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.090
products, You can turn it into soap.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.060
Now, how exactly does that work?

00:00:31.549 --> 00:00:35.860
Well, let's say if I had a
triacylglycerol just like the one here.

00:00:36.909 --> 00:00:39.400
This is what we refer
to as a fat or an oil.

00:00:39.409 --> 00:00:43.080
So, just like vegetable oil or butter
or different fats that you find on meat.

00:00:43.599 --> 00:00:45.019
That's kind of the same general idea.

00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:50.765
Now, if you add a strong base to
it, so let's say lye for example,

00:00:50.765 --> 00:00:55.565
or sodium hydroxide, or potassium
hydroxide, and you're to add those

00:00:55.615 --> 00:00:59.985
under very, very intense heating
conditions, you can break it apart,

00:01:00.015 --> 00:01:01.304
kind of like what your body does.

00:01:01.305 --> 00:01:04.004
Your body puts it through acidic
conditions and breaks it apart.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.360
But this we want to do through basic.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.399
So when that happens, you're
going to make glycerol.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.000
In this case, the fatty acid is going
to become a salt of the fatty acid.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.410
So pontification, just to kind
of do the official definition, is

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.310
when a fat is heated under basic
conditions to create glycerol.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.410
And the salts.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.930
Now the cool thing about this is this
is how all the different salts are

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.370
formed that you can find in your house.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.380
So from, for example, if you have a bar of
soap laying at maybe the sink, in a sink

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.510
or in your shower, that was essentially
made by adding sodium hydroxide or lye

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:43.069
to that, which that was originally a
saturated fat, so all single bonds.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.810
And that's used to make a hard soap.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.600
So if you have any of the ones that are
shaped like different things, That's

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.880
because it was a hard, a hard soap or
saturated because it was originally more

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.880
of a solid before it was being added.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:59.120
Now, if we add a little bit of a weaker
base, slightly weaker, like KOH, and

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.039
if this is added to an unsaturated
fat, this is how you make liquid soap.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:06.949
So like what you find, um,
maybe by your dishwasher or

00:02:06.949 --> 00:02:09.909
potentially like your shampoos and
conditioners and things like that.

00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:14.469
So like, for example, if you have like
a coconut shampoo or, or sometimes they

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.780
have avocado shampoos now, all those
are from oils that are unsaturated.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.090
So that's a little bit of a fact.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.989
Now, how does this reaction work?

00:02:28.455 --> 00:02:33.495
Well, the whole idea is, let's say if
I have this particular triacylglycerol,

00:02:33.495 --> 00:02:39.104
what you're going to be doing is breaking
apart by adding three moles of NaOH

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:41.015
under heat, just like I said before.

00:02:42.204 --> 00:02:45.394
And the whole idea with this is
that, well, we're going to break

00:02:45.394 --> 00:02:47.094
it into its different fragments.

00:02:47.655 --> 00:02:52.795
So, if we copy and paste this down below,
we're going to be cutting it with our

00:02:52.795 --> 00:02:57.544
little water scissors, just like how we
did with our previous hydrolysis video.

00:02:58.314 --> 00:03:03.855
But the only difference here is, so if I
were to cut it, where is it weakest at?

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:10.420
Well, it is weakest right where the ester
bond is for all of these structures.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:15.130
So we are going to cut it right at
this carbon oxygen bond because the

00:03:15.169 --> 00:03:17.730
oxygens are pooling in both directions.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.649
So it's ready to be broken under the
right conditions and basic is just right.

00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:28.470
So when that happens, right, we're going
to be pooling that into its two pieces.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:38.779
And now when that pulls, well, one side
is going to be our H from our NaOH, right?

00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:43.529
So if we kind of like look at this
structure and think NaOH, I like to

00:03:43.529 --> 00:03:47.209
think of this, this isn't completely
the right organikey way or, or

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:52.920
mechanism way, but it makes it easy to
remember is that with this one, we're

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.040
going to have two fragments, right?

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:55.750
There's H.

00:03:56.255 --> 00:04:00.525
and then NaO minus Na plus.

00:04:00.954 --> 00:04:03.344
So we have two charges on this.

00:04:03.855 --> 00:04:05.454
Maybe we'll use a different color here.

00:04:06.334 --> 00:04:13.425
So with each of these, so once I release,
erase the purple or erase the little x's

00:04:13.435 --> 00:04:19.855
here, just like so, we're going to put our
salt compound, because this is going to be

00:04:19.855 --> 00:04:22.665
a fatty acid salt on the right hand side.

00:04:23.384 --> 00:04:31.955
So we have our O minus and Na plus on
each of these different structures.

00:04:31.965 --> 00:04:41.575
So Na plus, O minus, Na so this is a
fatty acid salt, okay, which just as

00:04:41.575 --> 00:04:46.495
a reminder for if you don't remember
you're organic, this is also known as

00:04:46.565 --> 00:04:54.735
a carboxylate, okay, so it has an A T E
ending to refer to the salt form, now the

00:04:54.764 --> 00:04:58.795
other product is a glycerol that forms.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.360
And these are your two different
products in your final reaction.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:04.250
Okay?

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:08.680
So, when we look at the complete reaction
just like so, you're going to have your,

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:14.640
your big fatty acid, and it gets split
into the fatty acid salt and glycerol.

